course: dictionary
lesson: 14626
title: Posterior: back, later, or bottom
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Posterior is a long word, but it is useful in science. Look at the words around it, because they are its context and they give clues. «The frog's posterior legs are longer than its front legs.»
explain: Legs are part of the body, and the front legs are at the front. So posterior must mean at the back. A posterior part is the back part.
explain: Posterior has an opposite: anterior, which means at the front. If you know one, you can work out the other. Front goes with anterior, back goes with posterior.
explain: In a funny story, posterior can mean a person's bottom. «Ben bumped his posterior on the stairs» is a joke. Context tells you which meaning to use.
fact: Posterior means at the back or behind.
fact: Anterior is the opposite of posterior, and it means at the front.
fact: Posterior can also mean later in time, as in «the posterior part of the story», which is the ending.
fact: In a humorous story, posterior is a polite-sounding word for a person's bottom.
fact: In science, «the posterior part of the brain» means the back part of the brain.
exercise: «The frog's posterior legs are longer than its front legs.» What does posterior mean here? | Very loud | Very happy | *At the back | At the front
exercise: Which sentence uses posterior as a funny word for a bottom? | *«Ben bumped his posterior on the stairs.» | «The posterior chapters were the longest.» | «The posterior of the frog is at the back.» | «The posterior part of the brain controls balance.»
exercise: «The posterior part of the story is the ending, which comes last.» What does posterior mean here? | Very heavy | Lost | *Later in time | At the front
test: «The doctor checked the posterior part of the brain.» What does posterior mean here? | *At the back | Early | Very happy | At the front
test: Which word is the opposite of posterior? | *Anterior | Ancient | Luminous | Glum
exam: Which word means at the front, the opposite of posterior? | Ancient | Posterior | *Anterior | Barely
